remediation_playbook.how:
[MEASURED] this is probably your single most fixable tell: your source has luma sigma of 0.69 DN and warmth drift of 0.8 across 97 frames - photometrically frozen - while a real phone with a bright window in frame visibly hunts. Real AE and AWB servo continuously: when the subject leans, gestures, or the light shifts, exposure ramps over 0.3-1.0s and white balance follows more slowly.
Validated chain using sendcmd (eval=frame is required for eq to accept commands per frame):
  ae.cmd:
    0.0  eq brightness 0.000, eq gamma 1.000, colorbalance rm 0.00, colorbalance bm 0.00;
    1.10 eq brightness -0.028, eq gamma 0.985, colorbalance rm 0.025, colorbalance bm -0.020;
    1.90 eq brightness -0.030, eq gamma 0.982;
    3.20 eq brightness 0.006, eq gamma 1.004, colorbalance rm -0.010, colorbalance bm 0.012;
  ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf \
    "sendcmd=f=ae.cmd,eq=brightness=0:gamma=1:eval=frame,colorbalance=rm=0:bm=0" ...
Magnitudes: brightness excursions of +/-0.01 to 0.03 and gamma 0.98-1.02 - just at the edge of perceptibility, which should raise measured luma sigma from 0.69 into the several-DN range. White balance should move MORE SLOWLY than exposure and less often; AWB in a stable indoor scene might make one or two small corrections in 30s, warming toward the window then correcting back. Your source's locked +30 R-B warm bias is also worth neutralising slightly before adding drift.
Tie the events to visible causes: a lean toward camera, a hand crossing frame, a head turn. Photometric change with no visible cause reads as a bad grade.
Generate the cmd file programmatically from the same script that makes the shake, so events can be co-timed with camera motion.

IMPORTANT SCOPING CORRECTION: [MEASURED] Seedance's depth of field is genuinely good - Laplacian variance 504 torso / 230 face / 51 background hallway / 2.7 foreground board, which is real graded falloff, not a flat matte behind a hard subject outline. DO NOT add synthetic DOF; you would be fixing a tell that is not there and softening the image for nothing. What is actually missing is VARIATION OVER TIME.
Focus hunt in post, validated via sendcmd (gblur's sigma is not expression-capable, so sendcmd is the way):
  focus.cmd:
    0.00 gblur sigma 0.0;
    1.20 gblur sigma 1.4;
    1.45 gblur sigma 0.35;
    1.70 gblur sigma 0.9;
    1.95 gblur sigma 0.0;
  ffmpeg -i in.mp4 -vf "sendcmd=f=focus.cmd,gblur=sigma=0:steps=2" ...
  That is a realistic hunt shape: overshoot, correct past, settle. Keep the whole excursion under 0.8s and peak sigma under ~1.5. One or two per 30s clip, triggered at a natural moment - the subject leaning in or a hand entering frame.
For realism the hunt should affect the SUBJECT, not the whole frame, but a global gblur is an acceptable approximation at these magnitudes since the background is already soft.
If you use a conditioning still from a model that forces background blur (Nano Banana does this on every generated person), check that you are not stacking its bokeh on top of Seedance's already-good falloff.
